# Files that a Linux container or runner executes must keep LF in the WORKING
# TREE, not just in the blob. core.autocrlf is true here, so without these a
# checkout on Windows hands `sh` a script whose every line ends in \r — which
# fails as `command not found: <something>^M`, and does so only for whoever
# built the image, never for whoever committed it.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.dockerignore  text eol=lf

# The data packs live in Git LFS, and Pages is built by GitHub Actions.
#
# These two facts are COUPLED and must change together. Pages does NOT resolve
# LFS pointers, so serving `site/` straight off a branch would ship ~130 bytes
# of pointer text where data.ts expects gzip and break every version. What makes
# LFS safe here is that .github/workflows/pages.yml checks out with `lfs: true`
# and uploads real files as the Pages artifact — Pages never reads the repo.
# If the deploy ever moves back to "deploy from a branch", the packs must come
# OUT of LFS in the same change. See CLAUDE.md, "The packs left git history".
#
# Ordering matters: the LFS line is last, so its filter/diff/merge/-text win
# over the generic `binary` rule above it. `-text` is what keeps core.autocrlf
# (true in this repo) from ever line-ending mangling a pack.
*.gz binary
site/data/**/*.gz filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Vendored era client tables (build/sources/) — the expansion ladder's one
# source that no public archive serves. Frozen, committed once, and small
# enough not to want LFS; `binary` only keeps autocrlf away from them.
*.7z binary
